  nature         3       36        42      0.855   identical
  nature         5      126       134      0.942   identical
  nature         7      259       265      0.977   identical
  nature         9     1232      1232      1.000   identical
  brochure       3       33        42      0.790   identical
  brochure       5       85        95      0.892   identical
  brochure       7      231       235      0.983   identical
  brochure       9     1019      1021      0.998   identical
  nature_2048    3      129       151      0.853   identical
  nature_2048    5      483       508      0.951   identical
  nature_2048    7     1010      1031      0.979   identical
  nature_2048    9     5175      5172      1.001   identical

  median by effort: e3 0.853x  e5 0.951x  e7 0.977x  e9 1.000x

e9 at exactly 1.000x is the control, not a null result: `optimize_uint_configs`
is TRUE at e9, so the maps are still built there and nothing should move. The
saving grows as the share of encode time spent in entropy coding grows, which is
why e3 — where entropy is 51-61 % of `encode_inner`
(`benchmarks/e3_profile_2026-09-10.md`) — gets the most.

Against cjxl v0.12 this takes e3 at 1024^2 t=1 from 1.40x to about 1.19x
(1.40 x 0.853); that composition is arithmetic, not a fresh measurement, and the
ladder grid should be re-run before the 1.19x is quoted as measured.

BYTE IDENTITY:
  - 20/20 cells above, bytes equal.
  - 112 (image, size, effort, distance) cells re-run through the ladder grid
    (efforts 3/5/7/9 x sizes 64/256/1024/2048 x d {1,4} x 4 images): 0 mismatches.
  - 68/68 hash locks and 5/5 Libjxl byte locks unchanged.
  - Full `cargo test -p jxl-encoder --release` green.

WHY IT IS SAFE: `value_freqs` exists only to re-derive per-histogram symbol
counts under a NON-default `HybridUintConfig`. Below effort 9 the config is
always the default {4, 2, 0} — the same one `encode_token_value` already used to
fill `data.histograms` — so the clustered histograms ARE those counts. Verified
symbol-for-symbol before the change with a temporary probe over every clustered
histogram on real content: 0 mismatches, the only difference being the trailing
zeros `Histogram` pads to `HISTOGRAM_ROUNDING`.

That padding is trimmed on the new path so `counts.len()` (which feeds
`log_alpha_size`) cannot move. Measured honestly: removing the trim is
byte-identical on 144 real encodes and the unit test does not discriminate it
either, so the trim is defensive rather than load-bearing — it is kept because
it makes the two paths equal by construction. Recorded so nobody later "proves"
the trim matters from a green test.
